00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.490
starting off is with a nice hot pan so we're going to get the pan really hot and you don't

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:43.210
want to start start cooking in a cold pan when metal heats up it actually expands a little bit

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:48.440
and it'll actually it's porous it'll open up a little hole so if you put something in a cold pan

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:54.670
and then the metal will heat up it'll actually act like a suction cup as the metal expands whatever's

00:08:54.670 --> 00:09:00.000
in it will stick and that's how you get a problem with sticking so if you get a nice hot pan

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.440
And you put it in and you saute it.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.040
Another French word, saute, means to jump.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:09.590
And this is a saute pan, and you can see the sides of the pan are sloped.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:15.570
So if you really wanted to get fancy with this, you just work the food up the slope and it will come back down.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.030
And that's how you saute.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.450
And saute, like I said, means to jump.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.670
And the food actually jumps out of the pan on that side of the ridge.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.800
Another pan, a French pan, is a sauteuse.

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:30.000
And a sartus will have straight sides.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.480
It'll kind of look like the sauté pan or frying pan, but it'll have straight sides.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.020
And then obviously you can't bounce the food over, so sautus and sauté, two different

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.610
things.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:45.090
So we're going to sauté this chicken in a little bit of butter.

